{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -A 24 '\"'^## D44[89]'\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -56; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/rebuild_control.py | sed -n '33,64p'\"","aggregated_output":"4923:## D448 · 2026-11-13 · The wiring contract is settled; build and prove at $0, then review the code (council consensus under D439)\n4924-\n4925-- **Council** (`clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/mn-codex.md` to `mn-codex-3.md`, three rounds on `clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/WIRING-DESIGN.md`): the contract (inputs and authorizations the fresh path carries; one shared ledger reserving per actual attempt at every paid entry point, a persisted stop, a slice ledger handed off to the full run only by a separate decision; the $0 proof; the persisted run states and redo rules; what the run decides, with Stage B's six conditions under D428 and D429's every-judgement reading) is settled in version 4. Structural rule adopted: integration details are settled in code and its $0 tests, which the next review reads, not in further prose rounds. Consensus: \"correct the spending entries and Stage B reading requirement, then build and prove at $0 before code review\" (both corrections made in version 4).\n4926-- **Decided:** build the wiring and its $0 proof (no send); then a code review; the slice only after it, by its own decision; the full run only by a separate decision after the slice is read.\n4927-\n4928:## D449 · 2026-11-13 · The wiring's code review: four structural rules, two changes to the run states (council consensus under D439)\n4929-\n4930-- **Council** (`clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/mo-handoff.md`, `mo-codex.md` to `mo-codex-3.md`; Claude's notes\n4931-  `mo-claude.md`, challenges `mo-challenge-1.md`, `mo-challenge-2.md`): an independent read-only code review of the D448 wiring found eight\n4932-  defects, each confirmed against the code (a helper's internal retry could send after a stop; a killed child was redoable; an\n4933-  orchestrator's death was found only on its own relaunch; local stops were not shared; the ladder and judge reserved 1 × bytes instead of\n4934-  the agreed 2 × bytes + 4,096; launch inputs were not persisted; zero or negative usage could settle a paid request at nothing; the proof\n4935-  did not run the real orchestration). Consensus: \"agree to build and prove at $0, with the stale-registration relaunch race closed before\n4936-  implementation\"; the four rules close the eight findings.\n4937-- **Decided (rules):** R1, a stop is shared where it is decided (the ladder step's and the judge's final stops and local cap refusals stop\n4938-  the shared ledger), and every actual send in a budgeted process reads it first (a stop gate on `urllib.request.urlopen`, installed by\n4939-  each entry point; a 403 no component retries); an attempt admitted before a stop may finish. R2, the draw record holds its identity (kind,\n4940-  label, ledger, options; a differing launch is refused) and the outcome class (a signal exit is a crash); the launcher is alive while it\n4941-  holds its lock, registered on the ledger (the registration is checked under the ledger's lock before the launcher takes its own lock,\n4942-  and the gate stops the ledger when a registered lock is free, so an orphaned child's next send and every other draw's are refused).\n4943-  R3, every shared language-model reservation is the agreed bound, and the ledger settles a missing, zero, negative or non-finite charge\n4944-  at the reservation. R4, the proof runs the real orchestration with a trap in every child process.\n4945-- **Decided (changes to WIRING-DESIGN.md §D, not readings of it):** (a) the one redo of Step 1, Step 2 and production is a relaunch with\n4946-  `--redo` and a required `--redo-reason` written to the record, made only when the component's output shows an eligible failure (a\n4947-  transport failure after its own retries, an answer refused or not fitting its schema); otherwise the run stops; the ladder step and the\n4948-  judge have no outer redo (the judge keeps its own inside `run_stage_b`). (b) A crash stops the whole run. `run_stage_b.py`'s inner\n4949-  path (a first folder with no outcome, then the redo folder) is unreachable through `run_fresh.py` and is not claimed to satisfy the crash\n4950-  rule on its own.\n4951-- **Done ($0):** `run_fresh.py`, `budget_replay.py`, the hooks, the entry points; `test_run_fresh.py` 23 tests,\n4952-  `test_shared_ledger_hooks.py` 5, `check_budget.py` 16 checks, with mutants for each new rule; `proof_wiring.py` 10 of 10 (a wrong ladder\n    33\tdef rows(conn, sql, args=()):\n    34\t    return [tuple(r) for r in conn.execute(sql, args)]\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\tdef compare(rebuilt_path, last_seq):\n    38\t    rebuilt = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{rebuilt_path}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    39\t    stored = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{OLD2}/views.sqlite?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    40\t    checks = {\n    41\t        'views': ('select view_id, question, created_seq from views where created_seq <= ? order by view_id', (last_seq,)),\n    42\t        'view_keys': ('select view_id, measure, scope, period_start, period_end, unit from view_keys where seq <= ? order by view_id', (last_seq,)),\n    43\t        'evidence': ('select evidence_id, quote, created_seq from evidence where created_seq <= ? order by evidence_id', (last_seq,)),\n    44\t        'evidence_links': ('select evidence_id, view_id, seq from evidence_links where seq <= ? order by evidence_id, view_id', (last_seq,)),\n    45\t        'judgements': ('select view_id, seq, rung, reasons, cited_evidence_json, cited_views_json, changed, reference, implied_outcome, request_sha256 from judgements where seq <= ? order by judgement_id', (last_seq,)),\n    46\t        'rungs': ('select r.view_id, r.position, r.label, r.condition, r.lo, r.hi from rungs r join views v using(view_id) where v.created_seq <= ? order by r.view_id, r.position', (last_seq,)),\n    47\t        'view_switches': ('select view_id, open, reason, after_seq from view_switches where after_seq <= ? order by id', (last_seq,)),\n    48\t        'successions': ('select view_id, succeeds, seq from successions where seq <= ? order by id', (last_seq,)),\n    49\t        'dependencies': ('select view_id, depends_on, seq from dependencies where seq <= ? order by dependency_id', (last_seq,)),\n    50\t    }\n    51\t    ok = True\n    52\t    for name, (sql, args) in checks.items():\n    53\t        a, b = rows(rebuilt, sql, args), rows(stored, sql, args)\n    54\t        same = a == b\n    55\t        ok = ok and same\n    56\t        print(f'{name}: rebuilt {len(a)} stored {len(b)} {\"equal\" if same else \"DIFFER\"}')\n    57\t        if not same:\n    58\t            for x, y in zip(a, b):\n    59\t                if x != y:\n    60\t                    print('  first difference:', str(x)[:200], '|', str(y)[:200])\n    61\t                    break\n    62\t    return ok\n    63\t\n    64\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'linked-v9|Current conclusions|Comparison point|forecast|Previous conclusion|\"'^'\"\\\\[Themes' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/linked-v9-0710-request.txt; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/TEST-PLAN.md | sed -n '42,70p'; rg -n 'part_a\\\\(|case .*judge|claude.*audit' scripts/preflight.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'linked-v9|Current conclusions|Comparison point|forecast|Previous conclusion|\"'^'\"\\\\[Themes' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/linked-v9-0710-request.txt; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/TEST-PLAN.md | sed -n '42,70p'; rg -n 'part_a\\\\(|case .*judge|claude.*audit' scripts/preflight.py\"","aggregated_output":"170:[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n172:    Comparison point stated by that judgement: P|linked-A|v14|1\n173:    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): F|linked-A|v14|1\n176:[Themes in force, 3]\n179:- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 3. That forecast implies spending just above $1 trillion, but does not establish the $1.1 trillion threshold for rung 4. The qualitative power constraint is downside pressure, but supplies no quantifie\n181:[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The supplied evidence has no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or NVIDIA FY2027 segment guidance, so there is no direct reference rung to anchor to. Q1’s $75.25 billion growing 21% sequentially implies about $410 billion for FY2027 if that sequential pace compounds through the four quarters. I put the forecast modestly above that run-rate, at about $420 billion, reflecting management’s guidance for accelerating Q2 revenue growth and its $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility across two calendar years; the latter is discounted because it spans products and periods rather than specifying FY2027 Data Center revenue. This remains below the $450 billion boundary for rung 1. Memory supply constraints and China competition temper the upside, while the positive demand and capex themes suppo\n    42\t**The allow-list, in front of the budget transport:** every request that misses the archive reaches a wrapper before the budget\n    43\ttransport. The judging-scope wrapper passes only a judge request whose user text begins \"Thesis v9 (\" or \"Thesis v14 (\"; the item-scope\n    44\twrapper, built by the serial item runner for one item, passes only Jev requests whose questions are all link questions (the READS and\n    45\tSIGN builders' keys), and only when that item's sequence is 148, in the linked arm. Anything else raises before any reservation, which\n    46\tstops the sequence. The wrapper counts, per target judgement, the provider attempts; the harness asserts\n    47\tthat every v9 and v14 judgement of 07-10..07-12 was answered by a provider attempt of its own sequence (fresh), never by the archive.\n    48\t\n    49\t**The out-of-pair stop at inspection:** when `round.run` returns successfully for seq 148, the serial item runner reads the store's\n    50\t`dependencies` rows with `kind='reads'` before it starts the next item or yields to the day's judging: any row whose reader is not v9\n    51\tor v14 stops the sequence there (not comparable), whether or not that reader would ever be judged.\n    52\t\n    53\t**Transport:** the shared reservation ledger of the comparison runs (`clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py`\n    54\t`SharedLedger`, `BudgetTransport`, with `install_stop_gate`), one ceiling of **$0.15** for all six sequences, used as it behaves: a\n    55\tLuna call reserves its worst case per attempt and settles at its reported usage; a Jev call reserves its whole internal retry bundle\n    56\tand settles at up to the attempts times the reported usage, or at the reservation when usage is unavailable. After a sequence stops,\n    57\tthe shared ledger is read: a refused reservation makes the comparison **inconclusive**, even where `round.run` reports it as a matching\n    58\tor judging failure; an overrun or an unknown charge is reported from the ledger.\n    59\t\n    60\t**Expected new requests:** the v9 and v14 judge calls, the link questions of the item at seq 148 (linked arm), and a v9 judge call\n    61\ton any day it is queued by a changed v14. **Any other archive miss** (extraction, matching, another question's judgement) stops that\n    62\tsequence and is reported. Registered now for extra links the real selection may write besides \"v9 reads v14\" (for example with the\n    63\tstand-alone CPU question v8): the selection is kept and reported; a link whose reader is v9 or v14 changes a scored request and the\n    64\tsequence is scored as it ran, with the extra link named in the report; a link whose reader is any other question stops the sequence\n    65\tat inspection (above), and the sequence counts as not comparable. A missing \"v9\n    66\treads v14\", or v14 reading v9, is a selection miss.\n    67\t\n    68\t## Reading rule (frozen before the send; council R round 2, `codex-r2.md` §7(b), with round 3)\n    69\t- **A. Comparable answers:** both 07-12 answers give a FY2027 forecast in USD for their own quantity (a range by its stated ends);\n    70\t  quarters recorded where both give comparable figures; a missing annual figure leaves reconciliation untested.\n59:def part_a(archive_conn):\n340:    part_a(archive_conn)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
