{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/linked-v9-0710-request.txt | sed -n '1,16p;166,184p'; shasum -a 256 docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tThesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center segment revenue\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): How much Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n     2\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $450 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $400 billion and below $450 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $350 billion and below $400 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is at least $300 billion and below $350 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2027 Data Center segment revenue is below $300 billion.\n     3\t\n     4\t[Evidence in view under this thesis, 72 items]\n     5\t- e74 [fact; tradingview.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenue in FY2027 Q1, equal to 92% of total sales, up 92% year over year and 21% sequentially.\n     6\t    Quote: During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\n     7\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e122, e169; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n     8\t- e104 [fact; NVIDIA; original source NVIDIA and Nokia 2026-07-07; from gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08 2026-07-07] NVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to integrate Nokia anyRAN software with NVIDIA AI-RAN platforms for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks.\n     9\t    Quote: NVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia anyRAN software for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution.\n    10\t- e105 [fact; NVIDIA; original source SoftBank and NVIDIA ; from gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08 2026-07-07] SoftBank and NVIDIA reported a trial of stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform, delivering roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline.\n    11\t    Quote: SoftBank and NVIDIA recently reported stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform. The trial delivered roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline.\n    12\t- e106 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Palantir Technologies ; from gnews:1a116fecd5de1747cc5a06b1 2026-07-07] Palantir announced it is combining its new intelligence engine with NVIDIA’s Nemotron open AI models to deliver mission-focused AI for U.S. government agencies and major infrastructure operators.\n    13\t    Quote: Earlier this week, Palantir announced that it is combining its new intelligence engine and Nvidia's Nemotron open AI models to deliver mission-focused AI for government agencies and major infrastructure operators in the U.S.\n    14\t- e108 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] NVIDIA said its profit-sharing plan reflects a shift in AI demand from model building toward production inference that requires infrastructure to run continuously at scale.\n    15\t    Quote: Nvidia said that this plan highlights a transition in AI demand from model building towards production inference tasks, needing continuous running of infrastructure at scale.\n    16\t- e109 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon and Alphabet ; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] Amazon and Alphabet are extending access to their in-house AI processors, adding competition for NVIDIA’s AI computing products.\n   166\t- e210 [fact; TIKR author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] NVIDIA reported Data Center segment revenue of $75 billion, up 92% year over year; the quarter is not specified.\n   167\t    Quote: Data center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\n   168\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e201; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   169\t\n   170\t[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n   171\t- v14 (open; first_is_bullish) How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung 3 Neutral, judged 2026-07-10\n   172\t    Comparison point stated by that judgement: P|linked-A|v14|1\n   173\t    The system's forecast for it (our own judgement, not evidence): F|linked-A|v14|1\n   174\t    Reasons (excerpt): M|linked-A|v14|1\n   175\t\n   176\t[Themes in force, 3]\n   177\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-10; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the current status of no completed authorization, which sits at rung 2; no market consensus or company guidance on authorization by the deadline is supplied. The specific reports that China plans to permit selected firms to buy H200s, together with the report that officials are work\n   178\t- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 3 $600 billion to below $700 billion; judged 2026-07-10; reasons: The market reference is around $650 billion for 2026, which sits in rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). The evidence still implies about $650 billion: the 2026 estimates and NVIDIA management's cited projection point to that level, while the power-constraint view gives no quantified reducti\n   179\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-31) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 3 $900 billion to below $1.1 trillion; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits in rung 3. That forecast implies spending just above $1 trillion, but does not establish the $1.1 trillion threshold for rung 4. The qualitative power constraint is downside pressure, but supplies no quantifie\n   180\t\n   181\t[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The supplied evidence has no direct FY2027 Data Center consensus or NVIDIA FY2027 segment guidance, so there is no direct reference rung to anchor to. Q1’s $75.25 billion growing 21% sequentially implies about $410 billion for FY2027 if that sequential pace compounds through the four quarters. I put the forecast modestly above that run-rate, at about $420 billion, reflecting management’s guidance for accelerating Q2 revenue growth and its $1 trillion Blackwell/Vera Rubin visibility across two calendar years; the latter is discounted because it spans products and periods rather than specifying FY2027 Data Center revenue. This remains below the $450 billion boundary for rung 1. Memory supply constraints and China competition temper the upside, while the positive demand and capex themes suppo\n   182\t\n   183\tAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.cc876b60b8eef25b7675f6d078147d2e569ec97d1b1bfa969f9ec8060d596680  docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
